  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through next Friday)\nIssued at 1018 PM CDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nDuring the early part of next week, the upper low over CO\ncontinues to lift northeast, but precip chances linger across the\nforecast area, aided in part by another shortwave lifting across \nthe Permian Basin. The highest precipitable water values (nearing \n1.25\") will shift to areas off the Caprock by Mon, but similar to \nareas on the Caprock on Sun these areas will have received little \npreceding rainfall and the dry soils should limit the flooding \nrisk.\n\nThe polar jet remains active across the northern US next week,\nwith multiple disturbances evolving over and emerging from the \nPacific NW. Models have come into better agreement in showing a \nfast-moving wave ejecting onto the northern Plains late Tues, and \nthe associated sfc pressure falls will keep southerly winds in \nplace locally. Differences remain during the latter half of the \nweek but a SW flow regime is favored over the SW CONUS and W TX. \nThis would support occasional shortwaves and associated precip \nchances late next week into the Halloween weekend. The CPC 6 to 10\nDay Outlook (valid Oct 29-Nov 2) has a 60-80% chance of above \nnormal precip across west Texas. Beyond Tues, temps will generally\nbe in the 60s/70s with lows in the upper 40s/50s.",
